WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.140
All right, let's dive right into this high-yield review of annuities for the CFP exam.

2
00:00:05.140 --> 00:00:06.694
Welcome to this explainer.

3
00:00:06.694 --> 00:00:10.819
Look, we're going to treat this exactly like your master study sheet.

4
00:00:10.819 --> 00:00:17.513
We are prioritizing exactly what you need to memorize, understand, and actually apply so you can pass your exam.

5
00:00:17.513 --> 00:00:18.410
No fluff today.

6
00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:26.120
We're focusing purely on the critical rules, the formulas, and those really tricky common traps you are absolutely going to face.

7
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:29.109
We have six heavily tested modules to cover today.

8
00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:31.321
Number one, annuity types and basics.

9
00:00:31.321 --> 00:00:34.010
Number two, variable annuity living benefits.

10
00:00:34.010 --> 00:00:36.521
Number three, indexed annuities and QLACs.

11
00:00:36.521 --> 00:00:39.330
Number four, payout options and superannuation.

12
00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:41.781
Number five, taxation rules and formulas.

13
00:00:41.781 --> 00:00:44.949
And number six, suitability, ethics, and regulations.

14
00:00:44.949 --> 00:00:48.595
Starting right off with module one, annuity types and basics.

15
00:00:48.595 --> 00:00:51.105
Okay, before we get into any complex math,

16
00:00:51.105 --> 00:00:57.202
we really need to unpack this alphabet soup of how premiums are paid and when benefits actually start.

17
00:00:57.202 --> 00:01:00.131
Spell these acronyms out in your notes right now.

18
00:01:00.131 --> 00:01:05.211
A SPIA, or Single Premium Immediate Annuity, is honestly exactly what it sounds like.

19
00:01:05.211 --> 00:01:10.172
It's immediate income from a single lump sum with payouts starting within one year.

20
00:01:10.172 --> 00:01:14.954
Now, on the flip side, an SPDA and an FPDA defer that income for a year or more.

21
00:01:14.954 --> 00:01:16.448
So what's the difference?

22
00:01:16.448 --> 00:01:22.724
Well, an SPDA is funded with one lump sum, while an FPDA allows for flexible, ongoing deposits over time.

23
00:01:22.724 --> 00:01:26.849
Now, here's the absolute critical distinction you need to understand.

24
00:01:26.849 --> 00:01:29.897
The transfer of risk during the accumulation phase.

25
00:01:29.897 --> 00:01:35.635
With fixed annuities, you're transferring the investment risk entirely to the insurance company.

26
00:01:35.635 --> 00:01:37.428
They guarantee a minimum rate.

27
00:01:37.428 --> 00:01:43.047
But with variable annuities, the client keeps the market risk squarely on their own shoulders.

28
00:01:43.047 --> 00:01:51.116
Their funds are invested in subaccounts, which, you know, gives them a great inflation hedge, but it carries real downside market risk.

29
00:01:51.116 --> 00:01:55.001
Moving right along to Module 2, Variable Annuity Living Benefits.

30
00:01:55.001 --> 00:02:01.337
Let's clear up these highly tested and honestly very often confused riders attached to variable annuities.

31
00:02:01.337 --> 00:02:05.701
The exam loves testing if you know exactly what these acronyms guarantee.

32
00:02:05.701 --> 00:02:09.526
The crucial point here is comparing accumulation against income.

33
00:02:09.526 --> 00:02:16.818
A GMAB, or Guaranteed Minimum Accumulation Benefit, simply guarantees a minimum account balance at a specific future date.

34
00:02:16.818 --> 00:02:18.372
No annuitization required.

35
00:02:18.372 --> 00:02:25.903
Conversely, a GMIB, the Guaranteed Minimum Income Benefit, locks in a payout stream regardless of how the subaccounts perform.

36
00:02:25.903 --> 00:02:31.283
But, and this is key, it typically requires the client to actually annuitize the contract.

37
00:02:31.283 --> 00:02:37.499
Next up, we have the withdrawal benefits, and you absolutely must know the difference here for the test.

38
00:02:37.499 --> 00:02:43.536
GMWB protects the principal by allowing systematic withdrawals over a specified time period in years.

39
00:02:43.536 --> 00:02:48.019
But GLWB, the guaranteed lifetime withdrawal benefit, that one is for life.

40
00:02:48.019 --> 00:02:53.159
The client can withdraw a specified percentage every single year until they pass away,

41
00:02:53.159 --> 00:02:57.045
which is exactly why it's chosen by almost 70% of annuity buyers.

42
00:02:57.045 --> 00:02:58.240
It's hugely popular.

43
00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:01.169
All right, Module 3, Indexed Annuities and QLACs.

44
00:03:01.169 --> 00:03:06.668
Let's see how index products build on these foundations with very specific numeric triggers.

45
00:03:06.668 --> 00:03:11.569
You will highly likely see a RILA or registered index linked annuity on your exam.

46
00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:18.025
The test writers love making you calculate downside loss using either a hard floor or a cushion-like buffer.

47
00:03:18.025 --> 00:03:19.758
Here's how to think about it.

48
00:03:19.758 --> 00:03:24.600
If the client has a 10% floor, 10% is the absolute maximum they can lose, period.

49
00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:31.892
But if they have a 10% buffer, the insurer absorbs that first 10% of the loss and the client takes everything beyond that.

50
00:03:31.892 --> 00:03:33.326
Do not mix those two up.

51
00:03:33.326 --> 00:03:37.271
Now let's look at QLACs, the Qualified Longevity Annuity Contract.

52
00:03:37.271 --> 00:03:39.184
Why are these so heavily tested?

53
00:03:39.184 --> 00:03:44.324
Because they offer a really specific, really valuable exception to standard RMD rules.

54
00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:52.872
They actually allow clients inside qualified plans or IRAs to delay taking those required minimum distributions until a much more advanced age.

55
00:03:52.872 --> 00:03:56.518
Listen, you need to memorize these exact numbers immediately.

56
00:03:56.518 --> 00:03:59.925
Income from a QLAC must commence by age 85 at the latest.

57
00:03:59.925 --> 00:04:03.033
The maximum contribution limit for 2025 is $210,000.

58
00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:05.603
And here is a massive update from Secure 2.

59
00:04:05.603 --> 00:04:06.739
0 you have to know.

60
00:04:06.739 --> 00:04:12.298
That old rule limiting contributions to 25% of the account balance, completely gone, removed.

61
00:04:12.298 --> 00:04:15.406
Plus, the free look period can now be up to 90 days.

62
00:04:15.406 --> 00:04:18.394
On to Module 4, Payout Options and Superannuation.

63
00:04:18.394 --> 00:04:24.909
This is where the client transitions into the decumulation phase and decides exactly how to take their money.

64
00:04:24.909 --> 00:04:28.735
First, we have to define the underlying problem, superannuation.

65
00:04:28.735 --> 00:04:33.457
This is just the specific financial risk of outliving your money in retirement.

66
00:04:33.457 --> 00:04:35.549
Write this down in bold, right now.

67
00:04:35.549 --> 00:04:43.259
If an exam question asks you about mitigating the risk of superannuation, the answer is almost always going to be a life annuity.

68
00:04:43.259 --> 00:04:48.460
And this slide right here brilliantly illustrates the core trade-off of payout options.

69
00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:55.752
Basically, the more guarantees you add to protect your heirs, the lower the monthly payout you receive while you're alive.

70
00:04:55.752 --> 00:04:59.996
Straight life gives you the highest payout, but it stops cold at death.

71
00:04:59.996 --> 00:05:01.370
Nothing for the estate.

72
00:05:01.370 --> 00:05:04.658
If you add a 10-year period certain, your payout drops.

73
00:05:04.658 --> 00:05:06.869
Add a refund feature, it drops again.

74
00:05:06.869 --> 00:05:13.325
And joint and survivor, which covers two lifetimes, gives you the lowest monthly payout of the entire bunch.

75
00:05:13.325 --> 00:05:16.911
Okay, let's jump into Module 5, Taxation Rules and Formulas.

76
00:05:16.911 --> 00:05:21.872
Pay very, very close attention here because this is prime territory for exam traps.

77
00:05:21.872 --> 00:05:23.904
We have one right out of the gate.

78
00:05:23.904 --> 00:05:30.778
Unlike typical capital gains or FIFO accounting, non-qualified annuity withdrawals are strictly last in, first out.

79
00:05:30.778 --> 00:05:31.077
LIFO.

80
00:05:31.077 --> 00:05:36.636
This means all of your taxable earnings come out first and they are taxed as ordinary income.

81
00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:42.912
You do not get to touch your tax-free principal until every single dollar of earnings has been withdrawn.

82
00:05:42.912 --> 00:05:46.379
Also, do not forget the age 59 and a half numeric trigger.

83
00:05:46.379 --> 00:05:52.117
If a client withdraws early, hit the earnings, and I mean only the earnings, with a 10% penalty.

84
00:05:52.117 --> 00:05:54.807
The IRS never forgets to take its cut, right?

85
00:05:54.807 --> 00:05:59.110
This is an excise tax added on top of their ordinary income tax bracket.

86
00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:06.223
The principal, since it was funded with after-tax dollars in a non-qualified annuity, is never subject to this penalty.

87
00:06:06.223 --> 00:06:14.531
Now, if the client actually goes ahead and annuitizes that non-qualified contract, we use the exclusion ratio to find the tax-free portion.

88
00:06:14.531 --> 00:06:17.102
Just methodically follow these three steps.

89
00:06:17.102 --> 00:06:19.672
First, calculate the total expected return.

90
00:06:19.672 --> 00:06:24.035
Next, divide the original investment basis by that total expected return.

91
00:06:24.035 --> 00:06:28.040
Finally, multiply that resulting percentage by the monthly payment.

92
00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:28.339
Boom.

93
00:06:28.339 --> 00:06:33.001
That gives you the exact dollar amount that is a tax-free return of principal.

94
00:06:33.001 --> 00:06:36.049
Everything else left over, that's taxable interest.

95
00:06:36.049 --> 00:06:38.022
Watch out for these common traps.

96
00:06:38.022 --> 00:06:43.162
Under IRC Section 1035, yes, you can exchange an annuity for another annuity tax-free,

97
00:06:43.162 --> 00:06:46.509
or even a life insurance policy for an annuity tax-free.

98
00:06:46.509 --> 00:06:52.128
But, and this is a huge but, you cannot 1035 exchange an annuity into a life insurance policy.

99
00:06:52.128 --> 00:06:54.100
Do not fall for that on the test.

100
00:06:54.100 --> 00:07:01.632
Also, unlike stocks or real estate, beneficiaries inheriting a non-qualified annuity will not get a step-up in basis at death.

101
00:07:01.632 --> 00:07:04.919
Those embedded gains remain fully taxable to the heirs.

102
00:07:04.919 --> 00:07:08.266
Finally, Module 6, Suitability, Ethics, and Regulations.

103
00:07:08.266 --> 00:07:09.641
10 to 30, what is this?

104
00:07:09.641 --> 00:07:12.869
This represents the standard free look period in days.

105
00:07:12.869 --> 00:07:21.237
It's a really vital consumer protection tool, allowing a purchaser to basically test drive and cancel an annuity contract for a full refund,

106
00:07:21.237 --> 00:07:24.644
completely avoiding any of those steep surrender charges.

107
00:07:24.644 --> 00:07:25.780
This table is gold.

108
00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:30.920
When comparing a traditional IRA to a non-qualified annuity, the trade-offs are stark.

109
00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:35.044
IRAs offer tax-deductible contributions and early penalty exceptions,

110
00:07:35.044 --> 00:07:39.587
but they come with strict contribution limits and forced RMDs on the client.

111
00:07:39.587 --> 00:07:42.635
Non-qualified annuities completely flip the script.

112
00:07:42.635 --> 00:07:49.150
No tax deductions, no penalty exceptions, sure, but you also have no contribution limits, absolutely no RMDs,

113
00:07:49.150 --> 00:07:52.259
and they uniquely provide true longevity protection.

114
00:07:52.259 --> 00:07:56.741
On the exam, you have to distinguish which regulatory body applies to what.

115
00:07:56.741 --> 00:08:03.675
The SEC's Regulation Best Interest, or Reg BI, governs variable annuities because they are classified as securities.

116
00:08:03.675 --> 00:08:08.696
Meanwhile, fixed annuities fall under the NAIC suitability model at the state level.

117
00:08:08.696 --> 00:08:14.314
And overarching all of this, the CFP board code of ethics demands a strict fiduciary standard.

118
00:08:14.314 --> 00:08:22.025
That means duty of loyalty, full disclosure of any conflicts of interest like hefty commissions, and an absolute ban on churning,

119
00:08:22.025 --> 00:08:27.823
which is that highly unethical practice of just swapping policies around solely to generate fees.

120
00:08:27.823 --> 00:08:33.501
So as you're sitting there reading through some incredibly dense client case study on the exam,

121
00:08:33.501 --> 00:08:37.207
I want you to always bring it back to this one final question.

122
00:08:37.207 --> 00:08:40.136
Is this annuity truly the most suitable solution?

123
00:08:40.136 --> 00:08:44.141
Look, suitability isn't just about memorizing rules to pass a test.

124
00:08:44.141 --> 00:08:50.178
It's about solving the real human risk of superannuation in the absolute best interest of the client.

125
00:08:50.178 --> 00:08:52.270
Keep that mindset front and center.

126
00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:59.024
Memorize the triggers we covered today, and you are absolutely going to crush the annuity questions on your exam.

127
00:08:59.024 --> 00:09:03.268
What's the one concept from today that you need to go review right now?

128
00:09:03.268 --> 00:09:06.854
Get back to studying, and thanks for joining this explainer.

